      "writtenOn": "2024-01-11T16:29:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We definitely want `DEBUG_LIST` enabled -- this trivially catches list corruption issues that would otherwise allow for some classic exploitation primitives. I haven\u0027t done a performance analysis in a long time, but when I last looked at it, it was totally in the noise (there just aren\u0027t a lot of heavy list manipulation hot paths).\n\nThe question about `BUG_ON_DATA_CORRUPTION` is more about the policy on handling kernel issues. If we set `panic_on_warn`, then we don\u0027t need it (same result). If we don\u0027t, then it\u0027s a question of \"do we want the device to keep running in the face of a detected memory corruption?\" With my paranoid hat on, I think we should panic, but I can see there might be other opinions. 😊\n\nPerhaps @jeffv@google.com can answer the policy question?",
      "parentUuid": "f2e9af81_3dc3c624",
      "revId": "5fcb8ec01e7c85a58edc62544d45a3ba1aa78c0f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
